The Operational Alignment Engineer: A Theoretical Framework for a Modal Profession of Autonomous Systems Governance

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

Existing occupational classifications are organised around substantive domains and fail to capture professions defined by a transdisciplinary mode of practice. This chapter constructs the Operational Alignment Engineer (OAE) as the first fully specified instantiation of Profession 9, a modal supra‑category for professionals who ensure that autonomous systems continuously act in accordance with human objectives, safety constraints, and institutional values under conditions of limited, delayed, or impossible direct human oversight. Drawing on ISCO‑08, ESCO, competency‑based education, and alignment theory, we define the profession through five attributes: epistemic autonomy, systemic competence, knowledge generation, transdisciplinary integration, and strategic leadership. A three‑domain knowledge model is proposed. The profession’s operational core is formalised as a five‑layer Alignment Stack—Values, Objectives, Policies, Constraints, Actions—extended with the concepts of Architectural Shielding (a deterministic runtime enforcement wrapper) and Graceful Degradation Protocols (a deadlock‑resolving fallback hierarchy). The integrative competence multiplier $I$ is bounded by explicit boundary conditions that prevent dilettantism and ensure depth. A three‑pillar competency matrix, five proficiency levels, and a rigorous Fellow certification protocol are specified. The Frontier Professional Education Model delivers a four‑year spiral curriculum. Empirical validation includes document analysis, practitioner interviews, a Delphi panel, and a mixed‑method architectural case study that stress‑tests the Alignment Stack in a simulated multi‑agent safety environment. The Operational Alignment Engineer emerges as the profession that builds the behavioural immune system of autonomous civilisation.

