2. The Modal Classification Logic

2.1 The Limits of Domain‑Only Taxonomies

ISCO‑08 and ESCO organise professions by sector. An AI systems developer is coded under software engineering; a safety engineer under mechanical or industrial engineering. None captures the cross‑cutting responsibility for behavioural alignment in autonomous, learning, multi‑agent systems. The OAE must command AI/ML, formal methods, control theory, systems engineering, institutional design, and ethical‑epistemic judgement simultaneously. A modal overlay is required.

2.2 Dual‑Classification and the Two‑Dimensional Matrix

We propose that occupational space be represented as substantive domain × mode of practice. A practitioner can be dual‑coded, e.g., “Software Engineer (ISCO 2512) – Operational Alignment Engineer (Profession 9)”. The mode axis distinguishes routine execution, independent domain professional, and the Profession 9 mode of transdisciplinary alignment governance. A 2×2 matrix plots depth of domain specialisation against transdisciplinary integration, with the OAE occupying the high‑depth, high‑integration quadrant. The empty high‑integration/low‑depth quadrant represents dilettantism that certification specifically guards against by requiring demonstrable foundational mastery ($K_1$).

2.3 Jurisprudential Accountability

Traditional professional liability attaches to a specific domain act. When an OAE’s constraint architecture fails and an autonomous system causes systemic harm, liability spans multiple causal chains. Certification must therefore embed a malpractice regime: if a certified Fellow negligently fails to consult domain expertise or wilfully ignores stress‑tested failure modes, their certification can be suspended or rescinded. This creates the legal anchor necessary for the profession to function as a recognised public protection mechanism.

