7. Empirical Validation Agenda

The theoretical framework will be tested through a mixed‑method programme of four empirical studies, each designed to address a different dimension of validity.

Study 1: Document Analysis of Alignment Job Advertisements

A systematic content analysis of 100 job advertisements for roles requiring AI safety, autonomous systems governance, or alignment engineering will map the language of actual labour market demand onto the five defining attributes and competency pillars. This tests ecological validity and grounds the construct in real hiring practices.

Study 2: Architectural Case Study with Synthetic Stress‑Test Validation

To demonstrate technical viability to engineering and AI safety examiners, an architectural case study will be conducted using an open‑source multi‑agent simulation environment, such as an autonomous grid coordinator or a resource management loop with conflicting objectives. The 5‑layer Alignment Stack will be instantiated as a formal system design: a deterministic shield will be coded to enforce Layer 4 constraints, and graceful degradation protocols will be specified to handle constraint collisions. The environment will be stressed with scenarios including sensor failure, communication loss, and resource scarcity. The study will record whether the shield correctly prevents unsafe actions, whether fallback protocols resolve deadlocks, and how the alignment stack behaves compared to an un‑shielded baseline. This provides a rigorous, replicable demonstration that the OAE’s core operational logic is mathematically and practically viable.

Study 3: Qualitative Interviews with Candidate Practitioners

Semi‑structured interviews with 15 individuals identified by peer nomination as performing de facto alignment engineering will explore their competency profiles, knowledge integration strategies, and experiences of accountability across domains. Thematic analysis will test the three‑domain knowledge model and the relevance of the integrative capacity components.

Study 4: Delphi Consensus Panel with Accreditors and Policymakers

A two‑round Delphi study with 20–25 experts from AI safety, systems engineering, professional accreditation bodies, and large employers will test the feasibility and acceptability of the dual‑classification system, the certification tiers, and the bootstrapping protocol. Quantitative consensus thresholds and qualitative feedback will refine the framework for real‑world implementation.

Together, these studies will transform the theoretical architecture into an empirically grounded, technically credible, and institutionally feasible professional model.

