Appendix C: Formal Specification of the Alignment Stack and Architectural Shielding

This appendix provides a formal, implementation‑neutral specification of the Alignment Stack’s enforcement logic. It is intended to support the claim of mathematical enforceability and to serve as a reference for curriculum design and assessment.

C.1 Abstract Model of the Stack

The alignment stack is modelled as a tuple:

\mathcal{S} = (V, O, P, C, A, \sigma, \pi, \gamma)

where:

· $V$ is a set of values (normative principles, e.g., $\text{Dignity}, \text{Survival}$).
· $O$ is a set of objectives (quantifiable mission goals, e.g., $\text{MaintainOxygenLevel} > L_{\min}$).
· $P$ is a set of policies (operational rules mapping objectives to constraints).
· $C$ is a set of hard constraints (formally specified invariants).
· $A$ is the set of possible actions the system can take.
· $\sigma: V \times \text{Context} \rightarrow O$ is the value‑to‑objective concretisation function.
· $\pi: O \times \text{Context} \rightarrow P$ is the policy derivation function.
· $\gamma: P \times \text{Context} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(C)$ generates the active constraint set.

At runtime, the environment provides a context $\chi$, which includes sensor readings, state estimates, and institutional signals. The stack produces an allowed action set $A_{\text{safe}} \subseteq A$ such that all actions in $A_{\text{safe}}$ satisfy every constraint in $\gamma(\chi)$.

C.2 Runtime Shield Specification

The shield $\mathcal{R}$ is a deterministic, stateless (or bounded‑state) function:

\mathcal{R}: A \times \chi \rightarrow \{ \text{permit}, \text{deny} \}

or, in an intercepting architecture, a filter on the AI‑proposed action $a_{\text{prop}}$:

\text{if } \mathcal{R}(a_{\text{prop}}, \chi) = \text{deny} \text{ then } a_{\text{exec}} \leftarrow a_{\text{safe\_fallback}}(\chi)

where $a_{\text{safe\_fallback}}(\chi)$ is a precomputed safe action (e.g., hold position, maintain current setpoints, or engage the graceful degradation sequence).

Shield logic is specified as a set of first‑order logic sentences over the state variables $\mathcal{X}$ and action parameters $\mathcal{U}$:

\forall a \in A, \forall \chi: \quad \bigwedge_{c \in \gamma(\chi)} c(a, \chi) \implies \text{permit}

Each $c \in C$ is an invariant of the form:

c(a, \chi) \equiv \text{StateAfter}(a, \chi) \notin \text{Unsafe}

where $\text{StateAfter}$ is a deterministic prediction of the next observable state given action $a$ and current context $\chi$. Because the shield uses a simplified, conservative model of the system’s physics and institutional rules (not the AI’s internal representations), its correctness can be verified using standard model checking or theorem proving techniques, even when the AI is a black‑box neural network.

C.3 Graceful Degradation Protocol

When the constraint set $C$ yields an empty allowed action set—i.e. $\nexists a \in A: \forall c \in C, c(a, \chi)$—the system detects a Constraint Deadlock (CD). This triggers the Graceful Degradation Protocol (GDP).

The GDP is defined as a total order over safety modes $M = \{ m_1, m_2, \dots, m_k \}$, where each $m_i$ represents a progressively more conservative operational envelope:

1. Nominal Mode ($m_1$): All constraints active. Full functionality.
2. Restricted Mode ($m_2$): Non‑critical objectives relaxed; some hard constraints remain but with narrower safe envelopes.
3. Emergency Mode ($m_3$): Only survival‑critical constraints enforced; all optimisation objectives frozen; human authority alerted.
4. Minimum Viable Safety State ($m_k$): Actuators lock into a pre‑determined safe configuration; telemetry broadcast; autonomous decision‑making authority surrendered to human operators.

The fallback function $f_{\text{GDP}}$ selects the highest‑index mode for which a non‑empty action set exists:

f_{\text{GDP}}(\chi) = \min \{ m_i \in M \mid \exists a \in A: \forall c \in \gamma_{m_i}(\chi), c(a, \chi) \}

If no mode yields a safe action, the system transitions directly to the Minimum Viable Safety State and ceases all autonomous actuation.

Proof Sketch of Deadlock Freedom: Because the Minimum Viable Safety State $m_k$ is defined as a state in which all actuators are commanded to a safe static configuration that requires no further decisions (e.g., "close all valves," "halt all vehicles," "engage emergency beacon"), the action set for $m_k$ is non‑empty by construction—it contains the single "lock actuators" command. Therefore, for any $\chi$, there exists at least one mode with a permissible action, guaranteeing that the GDP never deadlocks.

C.4 Formal Verification of the Shield

The shield logic is amenable to formal verification using bounded model checking (BMC) or k‑induction. For each constraint $c$ expressed as a safety property $P_c$ (e.g., "oxygen level never below $L_{\min}$"), we verify that the shield, when composed with the system’s physical dynamics $D$ and any action proposed by the AI within the shield’s input range, satisfies $G(P_c)$ (globally $P_c$).

Because the shield’s decision is a simple Boolean function over a small set of symbolic variables (the constrained variables plus a conservative abstraction of the AI’s output), the state space for the shield‑plus‑plant model is small enough for BMC to cover exhaustively. This is in stark contrast to verifying the AI itself.

