Appendix G: Empirical Study Protocols

G.1 Document Analysis Protocol (Study 1)

Sample: 100 job advertisements retrieved from global AI job boards and professional networks, filtered using keywords: “alignment,” “AI safety,” “autonomous systems governance,” “constraint engineering,” “runtime assurance,” “shield,” “multi‑agent safety.”
Analysis: Directed content analysis using a codebook based on the five Profession 9 attributes and three knowledge domains. Two independent coders; inter‑rater reliability (Cohen’s κ) target > 0.75.
Outcome: Frequency and co‑occurrence matrices; mapping of real demand language onto the theoretical construct.

G.2 Synthetic Stress‑Test Case Study Protocol (Study 2)

Environment: An open‑source multi‑agent resource management simulator (e.g., a modified AI Safety Gridworlds or an autonomous power‑grid‑management sandbox) with configurable agents, constraints, and fault injection.
Design:

1. Instantiate the 5‑layer Alignment Stack for the environment: define Values (safety, fairness), Objectives (maintain load balance, avoid blackouts), Policies (shed non‑critical load before critical), Constraints (voltage within bounds, frequency within bounds, max downtime).
2. Implement an Architectural Shield as a standalone Python module that intercepts agent commands and enforces constraints using a symbolic constraint checker.
3. Specify Graceful Degradation Protocols: three modes—Normal, Restricted (shed luxury loads), Emergency (island microgrid, alert human operator).
4. Stress scenarios: communication loss between agents, sensor spoofing, sudden demand spike, conflicting constraint activation.
   Metrics: Shield intervention rate, constraint violation rate (should be zero), deadlock occurrence (should trigger GDP correctly), time‑to‑safe‑state, performance under unshielded baseline.
   Analysis: Comparative tables, state‑transition logs, and an architectural post‑mortem discussing the shield’s limitations.

G.3 Interview Study Protocol (Study 3)

Sample: 15 practitioners recruited through purposive and snowball sampling from organisations deploying autonomous systems (aerospace, energy, logistics, healthcare robotics). Inclusion criterion: peer nomination as someone performing alignment engineering functions.
Instrument: Semi‑structured interview guide covering: (a) typical work activities; (b) knowledge sources and integration practices; (c) experiences of accountability and ethical tension; (d) perceived competency gaps.
Analysis: Thematic analysis following Braun & Clarke (2006); triangulation with document analysis findings.

G.4 Delphi Consensus Study Protocol (Study 4)

Panel: 20–25 experts including AI safety researchers, senior systems engineers, professional accreditation officers, and policymakers from standards bodies.
Rounds:

· Round 1: Open‑ended questionnaire on the necessity and sufficiency of the OAE attributes, feasibility of dual‑classification, and barriers to certification.
· Round 2: Structured survey with Likert‑scale items derived from Round 1 themes, plus free‑text justifications.
  Consensus criterion: ≥75% agreement (4 or 5 on a 5‑point scale) or interquartile range ≤1.
  Outcome: Prioritised recommendations for framework refinement and implementation roadmap.

